The difference between 'find . | xargs file' and 'find . -exec file {} +' = The destination of both is assign every file type, but if the file name contains spaces or specific letters, xargs file will die but -exec find {} + accommodates for it
TRADE-OFF find . -exec file {} + (fix format '-exec file {} +'can use only 'find' but xargs can use with a lot)
Summary: find . -exec file {} + is better because it can handle every file name, but you have to use it with 'find'

The command find / in Linux searches the entire filesystem starting from the root directory (/).

grep "search_string" filename -> To search for a specific string in a single file
grep -r "search_string" /path/to/directory   -> To search recursively through a directory and all its subdirectories

strings command is a Linux utility that extracts and prints printable character sequences from binary (non-text) files.

Base64 is a binary-to-text encoding scheme that represents binary data in an ASCII string format using 64 printable characters (A-Z, a-z, 0-9, +, /).

Telnet(TCP port23) and SSH(TCP port22) are not the same; while both are network protocols used to access and manage remote devices via a command-line interface, SSH is fully encrypted and secure, whereas Telnet sends all data in unencrypted plaintext.

vim [name], chmod 400 [name]
chmod 400 = sets a file to read-only for the owner, no access for anyone else (-r--------).
chmod 600 = sets a file to read and write for the owner, no access for anyone else (-rw-------).

